====================================
EGCWCASE: The Chart Development Tool
====================================

Using a Windows development library for the first time can sometimes be a 
confusing and overwhelming task.  The concepts and terminology of a new package 
can be difficult to comprehend when you are not familiar with the software.  
A visual representation of what the software does and how each function 
effects the display can help to alleviate this problem.  To help you 
familiarize yourself with Chart for Windows, we have provided such a tool.  

EGCWCASE.EXE is a chart development tool that can be used to produce any of 
the charts provided in Chart for Windows.  It is an interactive, menu driven 
CASE tool that provides great flexibility in chart development. Using this tool to 
create charts is a great way to familiarize yourself with the different chart 
types and options found in Chart for Windows.  We suggest trying EGCWCASE 
before attempting to develop charts in your applications with our DLLs.  
Creating charts with EGCWCASE first, will make the concepts and functions 
provided in the manual easier to follow and understand.

EGCWCASE provides instant answers as to how each attribute of the chart is 
used.  You immediately see exactly what happens to the display whenever a 
characteristic of the chart is changed.  This helps in understanding the terms and 
attributes associated with a chart.  When you read about the chart messages in 
the manual, and use the messages in your application, you'll know exactly
how they affect the chart.

All the messages and attributes used with the charts are accessible through 
EGCWCASE.  Try them out with different values and see what happens to the 
appearance of the chart.  We suggest using the messages in the manual 
as a guide while you work with EGCWCASE.  As each message is discussed in 
the manual, try it out in EGCWCASE.  It will help you to understand what the 
message does.  

================
Chart Templates
================

EGCWCASE is based on the concept of chart template files.  When a chart is saved 
with EGCWCASE, it creates a chart template for that chart on disk.  A chart 
template file consists of the attributes and data for a specific chart.  Charts 
developed in your applications can also be saved to disk as chart templates 
using the WriteChart() function.  These templates can then be loaded into 
EGCWCASE and modified.  

This also works in the opposite fashion.  Any chart template created or modified 
in EGCWCASE can be loaded into your Windows application with the ReadChart() 
function.  The ReadChart() and WriteChart() functions are described in more 
detail in the manual.


There are a number of advantages to using EGCWCASE to develop chart templates.  
First of all, you can prototype and save charts in EGCWCASE without having to 
develop a Windows program.  This replaces the repetitive action of rebuilding 
your program after each change to the chart source code.  When you are satisfied 
with the chart template, it can be loaded into your Windows application.  At 
this point, any attribute available for that chart can be modified.  This is 
done by sending a message to the chart for the particular attribute you wish to 
change.  This includes the ability to change the data that is passed to the 
chart once it has been loaded.


For example, you could use EGCWCASE to create a template for a pie chart.  To do 
this you would select options such as slice colors, label styles, legend type, 
and other attributes from the menus provided in EGCWCASE.  It would take a lot 
more time to create a chart template if you tried programming to develop the 
chart instead of using EGCWCASE.  For each change made in a program, you would 
have to rebuild the application to see what effects a new message had.  With 
EGCWCASE, you don't need to spend time rebuilding.  This saves a great deal of 
time prototyping the colors and other attributes needed to develop a great 
looking chart.

Once the chart template is saved to a file, you can load it into your 
application.  To use new data with the template, simply call the appropriate 
chart message.  In this case, its the EGW_DATA message.  This can be done for 
any chart attribute and message.

===============
Using EGCWCASE
===============

EGCWCASE is very straightforward in its usage.  The File option of the main 
menu allows you to create chart templates, load/edit data files, setup the 
printer, print the charts, and save/load chart files.  

EGCWCASE uses a number of standard menus and dialog boxes for its interface.  
The File option of the main menu gets you started.  From this menu you can 
create, save and load chart templates.  The New option enables you to select 
the chart type you wish to create.  The column chart type defaults to use a 
vertical bar chart.  To change the column chart type for a series of data, 
simply access the Type option from the Series option of the main menu.

When a chart is created with the New option, a default set of attributes and 
data values are assigned to the chart.  The number of data series in a chart 
and the data values associated with each series are loaded from a data file.  
The data file may be given any name with the extension of ".INI".  This 
extension is used because the data file uses the format of a Windows 
initialization file.

The data file contains information to set up the number of series, number of 
categories, data type and the data values for a chart template.  Below is a 
sample data file.  

[EGW]
; Number of series in the chart
NumSeries=2
; Number of categories in the chart
NumCategories=5
; Data type to use for the data
; 0 - integer
; 1 - long
; 2 - float
; 3 - double
DataType=0
; The actual data values, use one line for each series of data with the
; number of data points equal to NumCategories. Use the data type as set
; by DataType
Series0=1 2 3 4 5 
Series1=5 10 15 20 25

To select or modify a data file, select the Read Data File option.  This 
option allows you to read as well as modify any data file.

The Open option enables you to load previously saved chart templates.  The 
dialog box defaults to use the ".IC" extensions for chart files, but the 
templates may be saved to any file name.

Once you have created a new chart or loaded an existing chart template, the 
main menu changes to add a number of additional options.  These options are 
headings for all the messages available with the chart type you have selected.  
Use the sections on chart messages in this manual as a guide when using 
EGCWCASE.  Any message you see in the manual is available through a menu 
selection. 

Messages of the same group will be placed under the associated menu selection.  
For instance, in column charts, all messages for Series, Categories and Values 
can be found under those menu options.  

Now that we have given you an overview of EGCWCASE, try it out and see what 
kind of charts you can develop with Chart for Windows.
